@meta
id: 26-04
title: The bang is an operator
chapter: 26
type: lesson
skills: filter-bang
par: 15
minutes: 5
vscode: select the import block by hand, then no command to run on it
open: registry.ts

@brief
Six side-effect imports at the top of the DI registry, in the order six
different people added them. The lint rule wants them alphabetical.

You already know how to do this with a range. Do it without counting
lines. `!` is not only an ex command — in normal mode it is an operator,
and like every operator it takes a motion or a text object and works on
exactly what that covers.

The cursor starts inside the import block. The blank line under it is the
end of the block as far as vim is concerned.

@keys
| `!ip` | filter the paragraph the cursor is in |
| `!}` | filter from here to the end of the paragraph |
| `!G` | filter from here to the end of the file |
| `!ap`, `!i{` | the same idea with any other text object |

@hints
Chapter 10 gave you `ip` — the block of non-blank lines around the cursor.
Put the operator you just learned in front of it.
--
Type `!ip` and watch the command line: vim has already written the range
for you, `:.,.+5!`, and is waiting for the command name.
--
`!ip` then `sort` then Enter, then `:wq`.

@solution
    !ip sort<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Press `!ip` and vim puts `:.,.+5!` on the command line. That is worth
looking at for a second, because it tells you exactly what the operator
is: a way of writing a range with a motion instead of with numbers. The
motion is measured, turned into line addresses, and the ex command you
already know runs.

Which means every text object you have is now a way of choosing what to
filter. `!ip` for a paragraph. `!i{` for the inside of a block.
`!ap` for the paragraph and its trailing blank line. `!G` for everything
from here down. `!}` is the one most people settle on for import blocks,
because it needs no thought about where the cursor sits inside the block.

Note that the filter is linewise whatever the motion was. `!w` does not
filter three words; it filters the line the word is on. There is no such
thing as filtering half a line — the command reads lines on stdin.

@start registry.ts
import './domain/order/order.module';
import './domain/customer/customer.module';
import './infrastructure/email/email.module';
import './domain/payment/payment.module';
import './infrastructure/queue/queue.module';
import './domain/refund/refund.module';

container.boot();

@expected registry.ts
import './domain/customer/customer.module';
import './domain/order/order.module';
import './domain/payment/payment.module';
import './domain/refund/refund.module';
import './infrastructure/email/email.module';
import './infrastructure/queue/queue.module';

container.boot();
